import json
R = [
[674,3,0,1909,"Little Mother, Pittsburgh","A girl holds a baby on her hip. In many poor families, older children took care of their little brothers and sisters while the grown-ups worked. Lewis Hine photographed families in Pittsburgh for a big study of working-class life.","Do you help care for younger children?"],
[676,3,0,1909,"Old Timers, Pennsylvania Miners","Three coal miners with lamps on their caps stand together. Hine photographed men who had spent decades underground in Pennsylvania, so people above ground could see who dug the coal.","What would it feel like to work in the dark every day?"],
[678,3,0,1911,"Breaker Boys","Dozens of boys stand in front of a Pennsylvania coal breaker, where they picked slate from moving coal for hours. The sharp coal cut their fingers. Hine's photographs were used to push for laws against child labor.","How many boys can you count?"],
[685,3,0,1909,"\"Follow the Gleam\"","A Welsh coal miner looks upward with the lamp on his cap shining. Hine called the picture \"Follow the Gleam,\" because a miner's lamp was his only light underground.","Where do you think his lamp is shining?"],
[687,3,0,1910,"Newsies at Skeeter Branch, St. Louis","Boys sell newspapers on a city street. Some of these newsboys were only ten or eleven years old and had already learned grown-up habits. Hine hoped his pictures would show how hard these children's lives were.","What would you do if you were a newsboy?"],
[690,5,0,1910,"On the Shore of the Lake, Kutenai","A Kutenai person stands beside a canoe with an unusual pointed shape, called a sturgeon-nose canoe, on a lake shore. Edward Curtis photographed many peoples of the Pacific Northwest.","What do you notice about the shape of the canoe?"],
[692,1,0,1910,"A Path Through the Pines","Sergey Prokudin-Gorsky photographed a sunny path through a tall pine forest in color. The trees glow gold and green, much as they would for someone standing there more than a hundred years ago.","What would it smell like in this forest?"],
[694,3,0,1910,"Addie Card, Age 12","Addie Card was twelve years old when Lewis Hine photographed her at a cotton mill in North Pownal, Vermont. She stands barefoot beside the spinning frames, looking right at the camera. Her face became a symbol of the fight against child labor.","What do you think she was thinking when she looked at the camera?"],
[701,2,0,1910,"A Cathedral by the River","Green domes of a Russian church rise above a town on a river in this color photograph by Prokudin-Gorsky. He traveled across the Russian Empire for years, photographing it in color for the tsar.","Why do you think he chose to photograph a church?"],
[703,2,0,1910,"The Royal Palace, Stockholm","Johan Beck made this color picture of Stockholm's royal palace at twilight, with a boat gliding past. Color photographs were brand new, and the soft hues give the scene a dreamy look.","What time of day do you think it is?"],
[704,2,0,1910,"Collegium Novum, Kraków","Tadeusz Rząca photographed the red-brick Collegium Novum, a building of Poland's Jagiellonian University, in color. He used Autochrome, the first practical color process.","How many arches can you find?"],
[710,0,0,1911,"The Emir of Bukhara","Sergey Prokudin-Gorsky photographed Alim Khan, the emir of Bukhara, in a bright blue robe covered in medals. The vivid color shows how bold the clothing of Central Asia was.","What colors stand out? How do you think they looked in life?"],
[711,0,0,1911,"Harriet Tubman","Harriet Tubman escaped slavery and then returned many times to lead others to freedom along the Underground Railroad. In this photograph she is an elderly woman in a white dress, near the end of her long life.","What do you admire most about someone who risked her life for others?"],
[712,4,1,1862,"Bloody Lane, Antietam","Soldiers fought here in a sunken road at the Battle of Antietam in September 1862. Alexander Gardner photographed the bodies still lying there two days later, and his pictures, shown in New York, were among the first to show Americans the dead of a battlefield.","Why do you think the public was shocked by this photograph?"],
]
